        "math_academy_id": "q-65230",
        "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x={sin}^{2}\u2061x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
